 "topics": [
  {
   "topic": "How identity reads specify missing-identity policy and how callers choose outcomes by provenance",
   "members": [
    "r1.00",
    "r1.01",
    "r2.00",
    "r2.02",
    "r3.00"
   ],
   "runs": [
    1,
    2,
    3
   ],
   "questions": [
    "What exact input does an identity read provide for missing identities, and does the caller select the outcome or does the store derive it?",
    "For every caller of an identity read, which missing outcome must it name, especially when a call mixes request-supplied identities, identities read from stored records, or optional bulk reads?",
    "How does a read-by-identity call name this policy, and which policy must each caller use?",
    "When an owning read such as `functions.read_contract_version` or `bindings.read_binding` is used both for a caller-supplied identity and for an identity obtained from a stored record, does its documented `unknown_reference` error remain fixed, or may it propagate `internal_error` or omission according to provenance?",
    "For each caller and read-by-identity use, which missing-identity disposition must the call name, especially when an identity was obtained through another module or when omission is allowed?"
   ],
   "units": [
    "50_public_apis.md:Conventions every operation follows",
    "50_public_apis.md:Relations of `store.read_records`"
   ],
   "id": "T1",
   "judgement": {
    "kind": "judged_before",
    "quotes": [],
    "later_state": null,
    "divergence": "",
    "why": "P3 already judges the same missing-identity policy input, granularity, scope, caller selection and validation question on the same texts.",
    "precedent": {
     "id": "P3",
     "round": "round-114",
     "topic": "Missing-identity disposition API, granularity, scope, caller selection, and validation",
     "kind": "answered_later",
     "quotes": [
      "Every identity query — by_id, by_position, by_attempt and spooled_files — carries when_missing (= models.MissingRecord), and the store alone decides absence by it: omit answers the records it finds and omits an identity no record has; unknown_reference raises UnknownReferenceError naming the first identity no record has, in the order given, and answers nothing; internal_error raises StoreInternalError naming it the same way. A caller passes unknown_reference for an identity its own caller named, internal_error for an identity a stored record names (a reference the store must hold), and omit only when it asks whether the record exists; it never checks the answer for absence itself."
     ],
     "why": "State 6 defines one `when_missing` field on every identity query and makes its value apply to the query's missing identities."
    },
    "verified": true,
    "blocking": false
   }
  },
  {
   "topic": "Outcome precedence and wall-time/resource accounting boundaries for pre-environment limit-setting failure",
   "members": [
    "r1.03",
    "r2.04",
    "r3.01"
   ],
   "runs": [
    1,
    2,
    3
   ],
   "questions": [
    "What exact interval and accounting boundary determine `resources_used` when the parent cannot set a limit before bubblewrap starts?",
    "From which event to which event is the reported elapsed wall time measured when no environment was created?",
    "If the wall deadline passes while the parent is attempting to set a limit before bubblewrap starts, is the outcome still unconditionally `crashed`, or does A03’s outcome order make it `timeout`; and from which event to which event is the reported elapsed wall time measured?"
   ],
   "units": [
    "50_public_apis.md:`public_op:sandbox.execute_function`"
   ],
   "id": "T2",
   "judgement": {
    "kind": "answered_later",
    "quotes": [
     "the wall deadline, the monotonic_ns reading taken when the environment starts plus bounds.wall_time_ms in nanoseconds and held in a local variable of execute_function only, after which — once a later monotonic_ns reading reaches it — it kills every process of the environment by sending SIGKILL, through a pidfd, to the environment's PID-namespace init, the child-pid bubblewrap reports on --info-fd — not to bubblewrap itself or its process group — and then waits for bubblewrap to exit",
     "both on the child after the fork and before it executes bubblewrap (a child that cannot set them never executes bubblewrap, and the execution is crashed)",
     "Returns SandboxResult with the outcome, outputs only for succeeded as PortPayload entries in output port name order (a value as a JsonPayload of its canonical JSON bytes, a file as a FilePayload of its bytes, a many file port as a FileListPayload whose contents are the tuple of its files' bytes in list order), resources_used with elapsed wall_time_ms, peak memory_bytes and the output count as output_bytes — for an execution whose child could not set its limits, so bubblewrap never started, the elapsed wall_time_ms and memory_bytes and output_bytes both 0 —, failure_detail, and detail_code None except for cleanup_failed."
    ],
    "later_state": null,
    "divergence": "",
    "why": "State 7 notes establish that the deadline begins only when the environment starts, while a parent-side limit-setting failure starts no environment, is crashed, and reports all three resource measurements as zero.",
    "verified": false,
    "blocking": true,
    "failure": "the deciding quote is not found verbatim in the later states' texts"
   }
  },
  {
   "topic": "Which parent-side limit failures count as a pre-execution no-environment crash",
   "members": [
    "r1.02",
    "r2.03"
   ],
   "runs": [
    1,
    2
   ],
   "questions": [
    "Is failure to set a parent-side limit a function execution exempt from the fresh-environment invariant, or is it a pre-execution failure that must not be described as an execution?",
    "Exactly which failed limit-setting operations trigger this no-environment `crashed` result?"
   ],
   "units": [
    "50_public_apis.md:`public_op:sandbox.execute_function`"
   ],
   "id": "T3",
   "judgement": {
    "kind": "contradiction",
    "quotes": [
     "Every function execution, trial and real, runs in a disposable isolated environment with bounded time, memory, output and processes.",
     "A fresh environment was created and, on every result but `crashed` with `cleanup_failed`, confirmed removed — except when the parent could not set a limit before starting bubblewrap: then no environment was created, the execution is `crashed` (A03 rule 5), and its resources used are the elapsed wall time with memory and output both zero, since no code ran."
    ],
    "later_state": null,
    "divergence": "",
    "why": "The first passage requires every function execution to run in an isolated environment, while the second calls the no-environment parent-limit failure an execution.",
    "verified": true,
    "blocking": true
   }
  },
  {
   "topic": "How flow proof handles deliberately missing references stored in a FlowVersion",
   "members": [
